hello and welcome to datatab  we have started to implement a new  feature in datatab  and now i would like to explain it to  you and i would be very happy if you can  give me some feedback  what problem do we solve with this  feature it is very complicated  especially at the beginning to  understand the difference  between directed and undirected  hypotheses  and especially to calculate them  correctly  to solve this problem we have started to  add a new feature to data tab  with this feature you can easily  calculate directed hypotheses  we started with the t-test for  independent samples  but if you like it we can expand it to  other methods as well  and now i will show you how it works  let's say you want to test the directed  hypothesis  that men earn more than women and you  conducted a survey  in order to find it out i simply use  this sample data now  i know it's much too small but it's just  an example  of course you can use your own data as  well by clicking  clear table now you want to analyze the  variable  salary and gender so you just click on  salary and gender and here is the new  feature of data tab  datatab will formulate the null and  alternative hypothesis  for you so the null hypothesis is  gender has no influence on salary  and the alternative hypothesis would be  gender  has an influence on salary and further  datadeb correctly calculates the p-value  for you  depending on whether you have a directed  or an  undirected hypothesis therefore you can  now choose  whether your hypothesis is directed or  if it's undirected if you select  directed you will be asked whether the  salary of  man or women should be higher in the  alternative hypothesis  in our example we want to check whether  the salary of  man is higher afterwards you will see  the newly formulated hypotheses  first the null hypothesis which says  that the male group has a smaller or  equal salary compared to the female  group  and second the alternative hypothesis  which says that the male group  has a larger salary than the female  group  the calculated p-value is now  automatically adjusted to this case  and you can easily read the p-value in  this table  additionally you can also look at the  summary in words  the null hypothesis that the mail group  has a smaller or equal value of the  variable salary  compared to the female group was tested  using a one-tailed t-test for  independent samples  assuming equal variances the result was  statistically significant  so the null hypothesis is thus rejected  if we now turn the whole thing around  and check whether women have a higher  income  we certainly get a non-significant  result for this question  what do you think about this new feature  is it useful for you  currently this feature is only available  for the t-test for independent samples  what should be our next step maybe edit  to  correlation analysis i'm happy about  your feedback  thanks for watching and have a nice day
 

